WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.103
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4520.

2
00:00:01.103 --> 00:00:02.207
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.207 --> 00:00:02.575
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Alright.

4
00:00:02.575 --> 00:00:05.149
You let us know when you're recording.

5
00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:05.885
And we'll . . .

6
00:00:05.885 --> 00:00:09.563
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Okay we are recording so can go ahead and start.

7
00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:09.931
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

8
00:00:09.931 --> 00:00:12.138
So today is September twenty-fourth, 2015.

9
00:00:12.138 --> 00:00:25.011
I'm Jamie Digilormo representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum in order to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

10
00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:29.425
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Center . . . recreation center on Old Spanish Trail.

11
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:30.897
And I'm here with . . .

12
00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.000
<v MCKENNETH EDWARDS>McKenneth Edwards.

13
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:32.713
<v DIGILORMO>McKenneth Edwards.

14
00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:35.208
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

15
00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:38.416
And this is going to be a short interview.

16
00:00:38.416 --> 00:00:39.842
Again, about ten minutes.

17
00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:50.178
And we'll hopefully get in touch with you at some point in the future to maybe get a little bit longer interview if that's something you're interested in doing.

18
00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:58.020
Alright, so I know you just stated it but one more time for the record, could you please state your full name?

19
00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:58.733
<v EDWARDS>McKenneth Edwards.

20
00:00:58.733 --> 00:00:59.446
<v DIGILOMRO>McKenneth Edwards.

21
00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:02.297
Okay, and when and where were you born?

22
00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:04.792
<v EDWARDS>I was born in Saint Patrick Hospital.

23
00:01:04.792 --> 00:01:05.149
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

24
00:01:05.149 --> 00:01:06.574
In Lake Charles [Louisiana]?

25
00:01:06.574 --> 00:01:07.287
<v EDWARDS>Lake Charles.

26
00:01:07.287 --> 00:01:08.000
1950.

27
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:08.415
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

28
00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:10.902
<v EDWARDS>And I was raised in Mossville.

29
00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:11.317
<v DIGILORMO>Alright.

30
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:16.707
So did your parents already live here in Mossville when you were born?

31
00:01:16.707 --> 00:01:18.780
<v EDWARDS>They already was living here.

32
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:25.000
I think Saint Patrick was the only hospital in this area at that time.

33
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:25.500
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

34
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.000
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

35
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.500
Either Saint Patrick or you had to go to Lafayette [Louisiana].

36
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.500
<v DIGILORMO>So you’ve lived in Mossville your whole life?

37
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.000
<v EDWARDS>My whole life.

38
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
All the way up until 1972.

39
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.500
That's when I got grown, got married, and left the area.

40
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.000
But I was living in Lake Charles.

41
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.500
I moved from Mossville to Lake Charles in 1972.

42
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.000
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

43
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.000
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

44
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.200
<v DIGILORMO>And just real quickly, what were your parents' names?

45
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.267
<v EDWARDS>My daddy was McKeever Edwards Senior and my mother was Thelma Prater Edwards.

46
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:05.733
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

47
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:08.067
So she was a Prater?

48
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:09.467
<v EDWARDS>Right, a Prater.

49
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:11.800
<v DIGILORMO>And what about your grandparents?

50
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.000
Did they . . . Were they from here as well?

51
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:26.267
<v EDWARDS>I'm not sure about my . . . Okay I know my grandmothers was

52
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:28.133
<c.p>from here.

53
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:31.867
<v DIGILORMO>What were their names?

54
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:36.533
<v EDWARDS>Anna Perkins and [Middy?] Braxton.

55
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:37.467
<v DIGILORMO>Braxton.

56
00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:38.400
<v EDWARDS>Prater.

57
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:50.533
See she was a . . . Okay, Anna Perkins Edwards and then Middy Braxton Prater.

58
00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:54.267
That was my grandmothers.

59
00:02:54.267 --> 00:03:10.133
Now Middy was raised in like . . . They used to call that area Saprack off of Evergreen Road.

60
00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:12.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

61
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:21.208
<v EDWARDS>If you go all the way north until around like Sam Houston Road.

62
00:03:21.208 --> 00:03:26.167
The Braxton’s lived back off in there.

63
00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:26.875
Okay.

64
00:03:26.875 --> 00:03:31.833
The Perkins live almost in Sulphur [Louisiana].

65
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.375
They call it Perkins' Town.

66
00:03:35.375 --> 00:03:38.917
That was almost to Sulphur.

67
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:53.083
So when my grandmother and my grandfather got married my grandfather bought the Perkins' home and moved it to Mossville.

68
00:03:53.083 --> 00:03:53.792
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

69
00:03:53.792 --> 00:04:00.167
<v EDWARDS>See, they moved it from Perkins' Town to Mossville.

70
00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:05.125
Alright now where my grandfathers come from?

71
00:04:05.125 --> 00:04:07.958
I really don't know.

72
00:04:07.958 --> 00:04:08.667
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

73
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:18.583
<v EDWARDS>Now I been hearing like Thelma daddy . . . His people come from somewhere in Texas.

74
00:04:18.583 --> 00:04:20.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

75
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.410
<v EDWARDS>Somewhere in Texas.

76
00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:27.048
Now my daddy's daddy . . . I don't know where his people started up.

77
00:04:27.048 --> 00:04:27.518
<v DIGILORMO>Hmm.

78
00:04:27.518 --> 00:04:30.807
Did you know them very well or . . .?

79
00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:37.855
<v EDWARDS>I knew my grandfathers but I was too young to get to know my grandmothers.

80
00:04:37.855 --> 00:04:38.325
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

81
00:04:38.325 --> 00:04:43.964
<v EDWARDS>Yeah I think one of them died before I was even born.

82
00:04:43.964 --> 00:04:46.313
That was my mama's mama.

83
00:04:46.313 --> 00:04:52.422
And my daddy's mama died when I was like about one year old.

84
00:04:52.422 --> 00:04:52.892
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

85
00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:55.241
<v EDWARDS>So I didn't know them.

86
00:04:55.241 --> 00:04:59.000
But I knew both of my grandfathers.

87
00:04:59.000 --> 00:04:59.500
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

88
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.000
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

89
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.500
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

90
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.500
So where did your family live?

91
00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.500
On a . . . Did they live on Prater Road?

92
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.500
Or did you . . . Where did you grow up?

93
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.500
<v EDWARDS>Okay I was raised on Old Spanish Trail.

94
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

95
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.500
<v EDWARDS>I'm trying to think of that street.

96
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.500
But it's Old Spanish Trail and Moss Avenue.

97
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.500
Or right now they call it East Burton.

98
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.500
East Burton.

99
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:35.500
On the corner of East Burton and Moss Avenue, which is Old Spanish Trail.

100
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:40.500
Maybe a half a mile from here on the left.

101
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.500
I was raised there.

102
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.000
And we used to play down here.

103
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Swim a lot in the pool.

104
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.500
Every Sunday . . . Well every weekend.

105
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.000
In the summertime every day.

106
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

107
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.512
<v EDWARDS>This place be full.

108
00:05:57.512 --> 00:06:01.907
Everyday playing ball, swimming, just doing whatever.

109
00:06:01.907 --> 00:06:03.163
Every year.

110
00:06:03.163 --> 00:06:08.814
Mossville was an active town for a long time.

111
00:06:08.814 --> 00:06:18.860
Until the late sixties . . . Okay, I was a part of the last graduating class of Mossville.

112
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.116
<v DIGILORMO>In ‘69?

113
00:06:20.116 --> 00:06:21.372
<v EDWARDS>In ‘69.

114
00:06:21.372 --> 00:06:30.163
So ‘69, and ‘70 things started getting quiet when they split the community up.

115
00:06:30.163 --> 00:06:38.326
Some students had to go to Sulphur, some had to go to Westlake.

116
00:06:38.326 --> 00:06:46.488
And for now I guess the community just got . . . How you say that?

117
00:06:46.488 --> 00:06:47.116
Disoriented?

118
00:06:47.116 --> 00:06:54.651
And everybody just started losing what they had; what this place had.

119
00:06:54.651 --> 00:06:59.047
And you know we had a lot.

120
00:06:59.047 --> 00:07:02.814
We had a good sports program.

121
00:07:02.814 --> 00:07:05.326
The band was good.

122
00:07:05.326 --> 00:07:10.349
I mean you were just a close community.

123
00:07:10.349 --> 00:07:10.977
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

124
00:07:10.977 --> 00:07:16.000
<v EDWARDS>And we did a whole lot together.

125
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:24.986
<v DIGILORMO>What were your first . . . What are some of your first memories as a kid in Mossville?

126
00:07:24.986 --> 00:07:26.671
<v EDWARDS>So much, man . . .

127
00:07:26.671 --> 00:07:31.726
<v DIGILORMO>Or just maybe one or two of your favorites?

128
00:07:31.726 --> 00:07:33.973
<v EDWARDS>This area . . . Record hops.

129
00:07:33.973 --> 00:07:43.521
See this place here . . . We used to come and have what we used to call record hops.

130
00:07:43.521 --> 00:07:53.068
That's when you pay a quarter to come in and somebody playing some music and you dancing.

131
00:07:53.068 --> 00:07:57.000
<v DIGILORMO>Y'all had like a live band?

132
00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.167
<v EDWARDS>No it'd be records.

133
00:07:59.167 --> 00:08:01.875
<v DIGILORMO>Records yeah that makes sense.

134
00:08:01.875 --> 00:08:02.417
<v EDWARDS>Okay.

135
00:08:02.417 --> 00:08:06.750
They used to call it a record hops.

136
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:07.292
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

137
00:08:07.292 --> 00:08:07.833
Yeah.

138
00:08:07.833 --> 00:08:08.917
<v EDWARDS>Record hops.

139
00:08:08.917 --> 00:08:17.583
And every summer you had a lot of baseball teams: Pewee league, junior, and high school.

140
00:08:17.583 --> 00:08:23.000
Now we had all this going on here every year.

141
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.167
When school let out.

142
00:08:25.167 --> 00:08:34.917
Another thing, May Day in school . . . That was a big thing because May Day it was no school.

143
00:08:34.917 --> 00:08:43.042
Of course we had to go to school, but it was no classes or nothing.

144
00:08:43.042 --> 00:08:46.292
All day it’d be outside activities.

145
00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:47.917
Just different things.

146
00:08:47.917 --> 00:08:49.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

147
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.607
<v EDWARDS>And I looked forward to that.

148
00:08:51.607 --> 00:08:52.041
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

149
00:08:52.041 --> 00:08:52.475
<v EDWARDS>Okay.

150
00:08:52.475 --> 00:08:56.385
Another . . . some memories I had it was picking blackberries.

151
00:08:56.385 --> 00:08:56.820
That

152
00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.557
<c.p>was a big thing.

153
00:08:58.557 --> 00:09:00.730
We used to pick blackberries.

154
00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.336
This area was full of blackberries.

155
00:09:03.336 --> 00:09:04.639
All the time.

156
00:09:04.639 --> 00:09:08.549
And we used to pick them to sell them.

157
00:09:08.549 --> 00:09:09.418
<v DIGILORMO>Oh nice.

158
00:09:09.418 --> 00:09:18.107
<v EDWARDS>And of course Mom and them wanted their share so we had to take care of Mom and them first.

159
00:09:18.107 --> 00:09:25.492
We’d pick a lot of blackberries, fill mama up and then all the rest we’d sell them.

160
00:09:25.492 --> 00:09:28.967
And we bought some all-star tennis with them.

161
00:09:28.967 --> 00:09:30.705
Or something for school.

162
00:09:30.705 --> 00:09:38.959
It was a fun time because all the guys my age we looked forward to going to pick blackberries.

163
00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:42.000
And that was a joyful thing.

164
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.708
<v DIGILORMO>Where would y'all sell them?

165
00:09:44.708 --> 00:09:46.333
<v EDWARDS>On Highway 90.

166
00:09:46.333 --> 00:09:46.875
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

167
00:09:46.875 --> 00:09:55.542
<v EDWARDS>We get those gallon cans and we get some big rocks and we lean them up.

168
00:09:55.542 --> 00:09:56.083
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

169
00:09:56.083 --> 00:10:06.375
<v EDWARDS>For when the people pass by, they can see they big, pretty, whatever and they'll stop and buy them.

170
00:10:06.375 --> 00:10:11.250
Now one gallon can would cost you a dollar.

171
00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.417
That wasn't much for

172
00:10:13.417 --> 00:10:15.583
<c.p>blackberries at that time.

173
00:10:15.583 --> 00:10:19.917
But that was a whole lot for us.

174
00:10:19.917 --> 00:10:21.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

175
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.719
<v EDWARDS>So we picked the whole blackberry season.

176
00:10:24.719 --> 00:10:30.031
Then mayhaws . . . Well really it were mayhaws, blackberries, figs, pecans.

177
00:10:30.031 --> 00:10:33.750
Or "pee-cans" what they’re call them today.

178
00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.938
But we usually called them “puh-cons”.

179
00:10:36.938 --> 00:10:37.469
<v DIGILORMO>“Puh-cons”

180
00:10:37.469 --> 00:10:38.531
<v EDWARDS>Yeah pecans.

181
00:10:38.531 --> 00:10:42.781
And we used to just . . . Mossville had everything.

182
00:10:42.781 --> 00:10:46.500
Children stayed occupied with stuff to do.

183
00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:53.406
Now we all had to learn how to swim because of that pool.

184
00:10:53.406 --> 00:10:53.938
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

185
00:10:53.938 --> 00:10:59.250
<v EDWARDS>See that pool was here before Mossville High was built.

186
00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:06.688
So it was a guy named Mr. Philip Broussard I think his name was.

187
00:11:06.688 --> 00:11:15.188
But he'd come every summer and he'll have swimming classes and I learned how to swim.

188
00:11:15.188 --> 00:11:19.438
I was between seven and eight years old.

189
00:11:19.438 --> 00:11:25.812
Everybody in Mossville my age, we had to learn how to swim.

190
00:11:25.812 --> 00:11:27.938
That was the thing.

191
00:11:27.938 --> 00:11:29.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

192
00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:33.583
<v EDWARDS>So when one learned how, everybody else wanted to learn.

193
00:11:33.583 --> 00:11:34.042
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

194
00:11:34.042 --> 00:11:37.250
<v EDWARDS>So I mean the pool stayed full.

195
00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.000
Every day the pool was full.

196
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.375
In the summertime.

197
00:11:41.375 --> 00:11:41.833
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

198
00:11:41.833 --> 00:11:46.417
<v EDWARDS>This really was our hangout right here, in the pool.

199
00:11:46.417 --> 00:11:48.708
That's the furthest we went.

200
00:11:48.708 --> 00:11:52.833
Baseball stadium . . . They had a big field over there.

201
00:11:52.833 --> 00:11:59.250
Sometimes the teams go play in Westlake, some would go play in Lake Charles.

202
00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.542
But we had good teams.

203
00:12:01.542 --> 00:12:03.833
I mean, Mossville was full.

204
00:12:03.833 --> 00:12:07.042
It was full of children back then.

205
00:12:07.042 --> 00:12:08.417
Full of children.

206
00:12:08.417 --> 00:12:13.000
I mean we just . . . I just enjoyed living here.

207
00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.317
<v DIGILORMO>How many brothers and sisters do you have?

208
00:12:16.317 --> 00:12:19.220
<v EDWARDS>I have five sisters and two brothers.

209
00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:19.634
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

210
00:12:19.634 --> 00:12:24.610
What end of the spectrum . . . Are you a younger or older sibling?

211
00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:28.341
<v EDWARDS>I'm the second boy and I'm the fourth child.

212
00:12:28.341 --> 00:12:29.171
<v DIGILORMO>Fourth child.

213
00:12:29.171 --> 00:12:30.000
Okay.

214
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.545
<v EDWARDS>So two and four is my numbers.

215
00:12:32.545 --> 00:12:32.909
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

216
00:12:32.909 --> 00:12:34.000
<v EDWARDS>Two and four.

217
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.909
<v DIGILORMO>So your household must have been pretty busy?

218
00:12:36.909 --> 00:12:38.000
<v EDWARDS>Busy, yes. [laughs]

219
00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:39.455
<v DIGILORMO>What was it like?

220
00:12:39.455 --> 00:12:42.000
What was the house itself like?

221
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.161
<v EDWARDS>Let’s see.

222
00:12:43.161 --> 00:12:51.290
Well we was . . . When the TVs come out, that's really what brought us together.

223
00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:55.935
Sometimes we argued on what channel to watch.

224
00:12:55.935 --> 00:13:00.000
Of course, it wasn't that much channels.

225
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.806
But when Tarzan came out, everybody wanted to watch Tarzan.

226
00:13:05.806 --> 00:13:06.387
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

227
00:13:06.387 --> 00:13:08.129
<v EDWARDS>Howdy Doody show.

228
00:13:08.129 --> 00:13:13.935
I remember when the Edge of Night first come out.

229
00:13:13.935 --> 00:13:24.387
It was fun because we . . . My two older sisters, they were nine and seven years ahead of me.

230
00:13:24.387 --> 00:13:25.548
<v DIGILORMO>Oh wow.

231
00:13:25.548 --> 00:13:36.000
<v EDWARDS>So when me and my brothers and my younger sisters . . . The oldest ones weren't around us too much.

232
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:47.032
So they was getting ready to leave or they was going on proms or dates and things like that.

233
00:13:47.032 --> 00:13:50.516
So TV really wasn't their thing.

234
00:13:50.516 --> 00:13:51.097
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

235
